## A. User manual

### A.1 What the pipeline does

One command reads the customer panel, checks that it has the structure the analysis assumes, estimates every quantity in the report, draws the figures, and writes this appendix and the report as HTML and PDF. If the data are not as expected, the run stops before any estimate and says what is wrong and how to fix it.

### A.2 Requirements

- A terminal on macOS or Linux (Windows: use WSL, or set `COUPON_CHROME` for the PDFs).
- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), the Python project manager. Install it once with `brew install uv` (macOS) or `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh`. It installs the right Python (3.11 to 3.13) and all packages into a project-local folder, so no global Python package is touched.
- Google Chrome (or Chromium), only for the PDFs. The program looks at the macOS default path and then for `google-chrome` or `chromium` on the PATH. On Linux or Windows, or if Chrome sits elsewhere, set the environment variable `COUPON_CHROME` to the binary; without Chrome, use `--no-pdf`.
- An internet connection for the first `uv sync`. After that the pipeline runs offline.
- The data file `assignment_data_panel_obs.csv` in the project folder (it is part of the submission folder).

The versions used for the numbers in this document are Python {{ meta.python | str }}, pandas {{ meta.packages.pandas | str }}, numpy {{ meta.packages.numpy | str }}, statsmodels {{ meta.packages.statsmodels | str }}, rdrobust {{ meta.packages.rdrobust | str }}, rddensity {{ meta.packages.rddensity | str }} and matplotlib {{ meta.packages.matplotlib | str }}. They are pinned in `uv.lock`.

### A.3 Install (once)

```bash
cd path/to/01-coupon-impact      # the folder that contains pyproject.toml
uv sync                          # needs internet; creates .venv and installs the pinned packages
```

### A.4 Run

```bash
uv run python -m coupon_analysis
```

The full run takes about one minute (the PDFs take most of it) and prints six numbered steps. It can be repeated at any time; it produces the same numbers each time.

`text.md`, the source of the report, is edited by hand. The pipeline therefore never overwrites it (only `--regen-report` does) and instead checks it: every estimate quoted in `text.md` must appear in `results.json`, allowing for rounding. If the data or the analysis change and a number in the text is out of date, the run prints each stale number with its line number and context and ends with exit code 3. The guard has blind spots: it ignores whole numbers below 100 (week numbers, counts such as 2 of 11), years, a short list of design constants (500, 1,000, 3,000, 10,000 and similar), and anything after the heading `## References`; a stale value that happens to equal such a constant is not caught. It checks that a number exists somewhere in `results.json`, not that it belongs to the right quantity.

Exit codes: **0** everything done and checked; **1** `--check` found errors in the data; **2** an error stopped the run (data contract, missing file, Chrome); **3** the run finished, but `text.md` quotes a stale number or the report has more than three pages. With `--no-pdf` no PDF is printed, so the page check is skipped.

