## D. Regression discontinuity: the 500 yen coupon

### D.1 Design

Customers with week-33 spending from 1,000 to 2,999 yen received a 500 yen coupon by e-mail. It was not announced, so customers could not move across the cutoffs on purpose; this is what makes the regression discontinuity design credible. The data show who was offered a coupon, not who redeemed it. D = 1{R ≥ c} is therefore eligibility, the design is sharp, and every regression discontinuity estimate below is an intention-to-treat (ITT) effect of being offered the coupon (Lecture 4 slide 15). A fuzzy design or an effect per redeemer would need redemption data, which the file does not contain. The estimand is the jump of expected week-34 spending Y at the cutoff c of the running variable R (spending in week 33), τ = lim<sub>r↓c</sub> E[Y | R = r] − lim<sub>r↑c</sub> E[Y | R = r]. The identifying assumption is continuity of E[Y(d) | R = r] at c, so that customers just on either side of the cutoff are comparable. The estimate is local: it describes customers near the cutoff only. Spending is recorded in whole yen, which creates repeated values of the running variable; `rdrobust` adjusts for these mass points, and there are many distinct values near each cutoff (the Lecture 4 slides (05-regression-discontinuity.pdf) warn about running variables with few distinct values).

Two outcomes are used. The level of week-34 spending is the direct outcome. The change from the baseline (week 34 minus the mean of weeks 30 to 32) removes customer-specific levels and is the more conservative one if the baseline is slightly higher just above the cutoff.

### D.2 Results

{{ tab:rd_effects | Regression discontinuity estimates (rdrobust: local linear, triangular kernel, MSE-optimal bandwidth, bias-corrected estimate with robust confidence interval). The effect at 3,000 is the coupon effect, that is minus the jump. The estimates at 10,000 are shown only to document that the design fails there (Part E). }}

At 1,000 yen the offer of the coupon raises week-34 spending by {{ rd.at1000.effects.s34.effect | signed }} yen (95% CI {{ rd.at1000.effects.s34.ci_lo | int }} to {{ rd.at1000.effects.s34.ci_hi | int }}), or {{ rd.at1000.effects.d34_pre.effect | signed }} yen relative to the baseline. Both point estimates exceed the 500 yen face value, and both intervals exclude zero. The change from baseline is the less stable of the two: it moves with the bandwidth and the polynomial order, and its interval includes zero in {{ rd.at1000.robustness.summary.d34_pre.n_ci_includes_zero | int }} of {{ rd.at1000.robustness.summary.d34_pre.n_specs | int }} variants ({{ ref:robustness }}). At the upper edge, 3,000 yen, the effect is {{ rd.at3000.effects.s34.effect | signed }} yen (CI {{ rd.at3000.effects.s34.ci_lo | int }} to {{ rd.at3000.effects.s34.ci_hi | int }}) in levels and {{ rd.at3000.effects.d34_pre.effect | signed }} yen relative to baseline; the data cannot distinguish it from zero.

{{ fig:rd1000 | Regression discontinuity plot at 1,000 yen. Dots are bin means of week-34 spending (a) and of the change from baseline (b) by week-33 spending; lines are the local linear fits inside the bandwidth chosen by rdrobust. The effect is the gap between the lines at the dashed cutoff. }}

{{ fig:rd3000 | Regression discontinuity plot at 3,000 yen. Customers left of the cutoff receive the 500 yen coupon; the coupon effect is minus the gap. }}

### D.3 What this does and does not show

The offer of the 500 yen coupon raises spending for customers just above 1,000 yen and shows no effect for customers just below 3,000 yen. The design identifies the effect at each cutoff and nowhere in between. Applying the effect at 1,000 yen to all {{ descriptives.group_table.500 yen coupon.n | int }} eligible customers is an upper bound; the evidence at 3,000 yen points the other way.

## E. Difference-in-differences: the 3000 yen coupon

### E.1 Why the regression discontinuity design fails at 10,000 yen

The 3000 yen coupon was announced at the start of week 33, and customers were told to spend 10,000 yen within the week. Customers close to the threshold had a reason to move across it. {{ ref:density }} shows the result: the density of week-33 spending jumps at 10,000 (rddensity p-value {{ rd.at10000.density_w33.p | p }}), while the density of week 32 is smooth (p = {{ rd.at10000.density_w32.p | p }}). {{ ref:rd10000 }} shows the consequence: customers just above 10,000 spent {{ rd.at10000.placebo.pre.effect | abs }} yen less per week in the baseline weeks than customers just below, which a valid design would not produce (p {{ rd.at10000.placebo.pre.p | p }}). The baseline jump is the main reason continuity fails: a quantity that the coupon cannot have changed differs across the cutoff. The density test supports this, but it is neither necessary nor sufficient for a valid design (Lecture 4 slide 55), so it does not carry the conclusion alone. The regression discontinuity estimate of {{ rd.at10000.effects.s34.effect | signed }} yen therefore cannot be read as a coupon effect.

{{ fig:rd10000 | The regression discontinuity design at 10,000 yen is not valid. (a) The baseline already jumps at the cutoff; (b) the naive estimate for week 34 inherits this. }}

### E.2 Design

The comparison group is customers with 3,000 to 9,999 yen in week 33. They received no coupon and their week-33 spending lies below the manipulated region. The baseline is the mean of weeks 30 to 32, the weeks before the announcement. Identification rests on parallel trends: without the coupon, the gap between the groups would have stayed as it was in the baseline weeks.

This departs from the 2×2 set-up of Lecture 5, where the groups are fixed before the treatment (slide 19). Here G is defined by week-33 spending, which is measured after the announcement and is itself an outcome of it (E.4). The estimate α is therefore the average effect on the treated (ATT) for customers who spent 10,000 yen or more in week 33, including those the announcement pushed over the line.

The entry point is the Card-Krueger table of Lecture 5 (slides 8-17). {{ ref:did_means }} shows the four cell means with the notation of the slides. {{ ref:ck_naive }} lists the two naive comparisons and what each contains. The before-after change of the coupon group, {{ did.card_krueger.naive_before_after | signed }} yen, includes the change of {{ did.card_krueger.bias_time | signed }} yen that the comparison group showed without any coupon: a time effect. The comparison of the two groups after the coupon, {{ did.card_krueger.naive_cross_section | signed }} yen, includes the gap of {{ did.card_krueger.bias_selection | signed }} yen that existed before the coupon: the selection bias term E[Y(0) | G = 1] − E[Y(0) | G = 0] of the slides. The difference-in-differences removes both, α = {{ did.card_krueger.naive_before_after | int }} − {{ did.card_krueger.bias_time | int }} = {{ did.card_krueger.did | signed }} yen. It equals the regression coefficient in {{ ref:did_means }}.

{{ tab:did_means | Card-Krueger 2×2 table: mean weekly spending of the coupon group (G = 1) and the comparison group (G = 0), before (T = 0) and after (T = 1) the coupon. The last row is the gap between the groups; its last cell is α. }}

{{ tab:ck_naive | The two naive comparisons next to the difference-in-differences. The comparison after the coupon differs by about 1 yen from the 3000 yen figure in Part C, because Part C follows the data flag for the one customer at exactly 3,000 yen and this table follows the written rule. }}

{{ tab:event_study | Event study: gap between the 3000 yen group and the comparison group in each week, relative to week 32. Standard errors are clustered by customer. }}

{{ fig:event | Event study. The gap between the groups moves little in weeks 30 to 32 (week 30: [[ did.event_study.coefficients.30.estimate | signed ]] yen) and jumps in week 34. The hollow marker (week 33) is not a result: the groups are defined by spending in that week. }}

The gap is {{ did.gap_by_week.30 | int }} yen in week 30, {{ did.gap_by_week.31 | int }} in week 31 and {{ did.gap_by_week.32 | int }} in week 32, and {{ did.gap_by_week.34 | int }} in week 34. The difference-in-differences estimate is {{ did.main.estimate | signed }} yen per customer (95% CI {{ did.main.ci_lo | int }} to {{ did.main.ci_hi | int }}). The pre-trend coefficients are {{ did.event_study.coefficients.30.estimate | signed }} yen in week 30 and {{ did.event_study.coefficients.31.estimate | signed }} yen in week 31. The gap is therefore not flat: the joint test rejects zero (p {{ did.event_study.pretrend_joint_p | p }}), and the week-30 coefficient alone has p = {{ did.event_study.coefficients.30.p | p }}. The deviation is small, though: {{ did.pretrend_w30_share_of_gap | pct1 }} of the gap. Extending this trend to week 34 would lower the estimate to {{ did.trend_adjusted.estimate | int }} yen, which bounds the possible bias from a linear trend.

### E.3 Robustness of the difference-in-differences estimate

{{ tab:did_robust | The 3000 yen coupon effect under alternative specifications. }}

The estimate changes little across baseline choices, covariates and the trend adjustment. The placebo applies the same design one step back in time, when no coupon existed, and finds {{ did.placebo.estimate | signed }} yen (CI {{ did.placebo.ci_lo | int }} to {{ did.placebo.ci_hi | int }}). That differs from zero at the 5% level, so some selection or mean reversion remains; it is {{ did.placebo_share_of_main | pct1 }} of the main estimate, and subtracting it gives {{ did.placebo_adjusted | int }} yen. The proportional version, which assumes that effects scale with a customer's own baseline, gives {{ did.proportional.att_yen | int }} yen; the size of the effect depends on this functional-form assumption, and Part G uses both.

The groups are defined by week-33 spending, the week the announcement moved. Two rows therefore remove the customers near 10,000: dropping everyone with 8,000 to 11,999 yen gives {{ did.band_dropped.estimate | int }} yen (CI {{ did.band_dropped.ci_lo | int }} to {{ did.band_dropped.ci_hi | int }}), and keeping all treated customers against controls with at most 7,999 yen gives {{ did.control_far.estimate | int }} yen. Neither design can be fully clean. Customers may have pulled purchases forward from week 34 into week 33, which would lower the week-34 effect, and the comparison group may have been nudged by the announcement too. The data cannot measure either.

### E.4 The announcement effect in week 33

The effect of the 3000 yen coupon includes what the announcement did in week 33. {{ ref:bunching }} compares the week-33 histogram with the counterfactual and finds a hole below 10,000 and a surplus above it. {{ bunching.week33.missing_mass | int }} customers moved from below 10,000 to above it, by about {{ bunching.week33.stretch | int }} yen each, which adds {{ bunching.week33.effect_total | int }} yen to week-33 spending (95% CI {{ bunching.effect_total_ci.0 | int }} to {{ bunching.effect_total_ci.1 | int }}). That is {{ bunching.effect_per_eligible | int }} yen per eligible customer, small next to the 3,000 yen coupon. The same procedure for week 32, when nothing was announced, finds a missing mass of {{ bunching.week32_placebo.missing_mass | int }}.

{{ fig:bunching | Histogram of week-33 spending against the counterfactual (weeks 31 and 32, rescaled). Shaded: response region of 2,500 yen on each side of 10,000. }}

{{ tab:bunching | Bunching estimate of the announcement effect in week 33. }}

### E.5 Donut regression discontinuity

As a second route around the manipulation, the customers within a few hundred yen of 10,000 are removed and the regression discontinuity design is estimated again on the rest.

{{ tab:donut | Regression discontinuity at 10,000 yen with a donut. The jump in the baseline measures how far the design is from valid. }}

The estimate for the change from baseline stays positive and clearly different from zero for every donut ({{ rd.at10000.donut.1.d34_pre.effect | int }} to {{ rd.at10000.donut.3.d34_pre.effect | int }} yen) and is smaller than the difference-in-differences estimate. This is what proportional effects imply: the regression discontinuity estimate describes customers whose baseline is around 10,000 yen, the difference-in-differences estimate the whole group with a baseline of {{ did.main.mean_treated_pre | int }} yen. The level estimate for week 34 becomes imprecise as the donut grows and is not informative. Because the baseline still jumps at the cutoff ({{ rd.at10000.donut.3.pre.effect | int }} yen at the widest donut), the donut regression discontinuity is a robustness check and the difference-in-differences estimate is the main result.

## F. Diagnostics

### F.1 Density of the running variable

{{ tab:density | Density test of Cattaneo, Jansson and Ma (rddensity), jackknife variance. T is the test statistic, p its p-value; the null hypothesis is that the density is continuous at the cutoff. }}

{{ fig:density | Histograms of spending in weeks 32 and 33 around each cutoff, with rddensity p-values. }}

There is no sign of manipulation at 1,000 yen (week 33: p = {{ rd.at1000.density_w33.p | p }}). At 3,000 yen the week-33 test gives p = {{ rd.at3000.density_w33.p | p }}, just below the 5% line, while week 32 gives {{ rd.at3000.density_w32.p | p }}. The 500 yen coupon was not announced, and one borderline result among {{ rd.n_density_tests | int }} density tests is not unusual. It is reported, and it is separate from the balance and placebo tests of F.2. At 10,000 yen the test rejects clearly (p {{ rd.at10000.density_w33.p | p }}).

### F.2 Balance and placebo outcomes

If the design is valid, characteristics fixed before the coupon and spending in the baseline weeks should be continuous at the cutoff. Eleven tests per cutoff form one family (five covariates, four baseline outcomes, two lag placebos). With eleven independent tests at 5%, the chance of at least one rejection in a valid design is 43% (1 − 0.95^11), so the raw count says little. The Holm step-down correction (Lecture 2 slides, deck 03, slide 10) controls the chance of any false rejection in the family at 5%. {{ ref:balance }} gives raw and adjusted p-values.

{{ tab:balance | Jumps at the two cutoffs in covariates and in outcomes the coupon cannot have changed (robust p-values), with Holm-adjusted p-values over the eleven tests of each cutoff. The lag placebos use spending in week 32 (or 31) as the running variable and the following week as the outcome. A dagger marks a test of a covariate that is almost constant inside the bandwidth: the member share at 1,000 yen has [[ rd.at1000.balance.is_member.n_rare_in_h | int ]] members among the customers within the bandwidth, so the test is effectively uninformative. }}

{{ fig:diagnostics | Jumps at 1,000 and 3,000 yen with robust 95% confidence intervals. Red: raw p below 0.05. }}

At 1,000 yen, {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.n_reject_5pct | int }} of {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.n_tests | int }} tests reject at 5% before and {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.n_reject_holm | int }} after the Holm correction (smallest raw p = {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.min_p | p }}, smallest adjusted p = {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.min_p_holm | p }}). The member test cannot reject anything, so the family effectively has {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.n_tests_informative | int }} informative tests; leaving it out changes the smallest adjusted p to {{ rd.at1000.multiple_testing.min_p_holm_informative | p }}. The baseline weeks lie {{ rd.at1000.placebo.pre.effect | int }} yen higher just above the cutoff, not significant, but too large to ignore; this is why the change-from-baseline outcome is reported next to the level. At 3,000 yen, {{ rd.at3000.multiple_testing.n_reject_5pct | int }} of {{ rd.at3000.multiple_testing.n_tests | int }} tests reject at 5% ({{ rd.at3000.multiple_testing.rejected_text | str }}) and {{ rd.at3000.multiple_testing.n_reject_holm | int }} after the correction (smallest adjusted p = {{ rd.at3000.multiple_testing.min_p_holm | p }}). The age imbalance remains the smallest p of the family, so it deserves a look in the null result at 3,000 yen, but it does not survive the correction.

