### F.4 Diagnostics of the difference-in-differences design

Parallel trends cannot be tested after the treatment; the checks above are the usual indirect evidence: the pre-trend coefficients of the event study ({{ did.event_study.coefficients.30.estimate | signed }} and {{ did.event_study.coefficients.31.estimate | signed }} yen: small, but the joint test rejects zero), the placebo DiD ({{ did.placebo.estimate | signed }} yen), the trend-adjusted estimate and the proportional specification. The week-33 coefficient is not a diagnostic: customers are sorted into groups by their spending in that week, so a gap there is mechanical.

### F.5 Limits

- **Local effects.** Each regression discontinuity estimate holds for customers at its cutoff only.
- **Intention to treat.** The data show eligibility, not redemption. The regression discontinuity effects are ITT effects of being offered the coupon. The difference-in-differences coefficient α is the average effect on the treated group (ATT); since redemption is not observed, G = 1 means eligible, so α is also an effect of the offer.
- **Spending is not profit.** See Part G.
- **One flagged customer.** One customer with exactly 3,000 yen is flagged as eligible for the 500 yen coupon although the written rule excludes 3,000. The regression discontinuity estimates use the written rule; one customer cannot move them.
- **Selection on one week.** The groups are defined by one week of spending, after the announcement, which departs from the fixed groups of the 2×2 set-up in Lecture 5. The placebo and the pre-trend checks address this, and no check can remove it entirely.
- **Bundled treatment.** For the 3000 yen coupon the announcement (week 33) and the coupon (week 34) come together. α measures both. There is no staggered start, so the problems of two-way fixed effects with staggered timing (Lecture 5, slides 37-42) do not arise.
- **No randomisation.** All estimates rest on the assumptions stated in D.1 and E.2.

## G. Cost-benefit

A coupon pays for itself only if the margin on the extra spending covers the coupon cost. The data contain no margin and no redemption. So the table shows the cost under the stated assumption that every eligible customer redeems the full face value, and the break-even margin: coupon cost divided by incremental spending. For lower redemption rates, cost and break-even margin scale down by the same factor. Incremental spending for the 3000 yen coupon is the week-34 effect times the {{ costbenefit.coupon_3000.did_levels.n_eligible | int }} eligible customers plus the week-33 announcement effect from E.4. For the 500 yen coupon it is the local effect at 1,000 yen applied to all {{ costbenefit.coupon_500.at_lower_edge.n_eligible | int }} eligible customers, which is an upper bound.

{{ tab:costbenefit | Coupon cost and incremental spending, in million yen, with the margin needed to break even. Spending, not profit. }}

For the 3000 yen coupon, incremental spending is {{ costbenefit.coupon_3000.did_levels.incremental_revenue | mil1 }} million yen against a cost of {{ costbenefit.coupon_3000.did_levels.cost_full_redemption | mil1 }} million yen at full redemption. A margin of {{ costbenefit.coupon_3000.did_levels.by_redemption.100.break_even_margin | pct0 }} would be needed to break even, and {{ costbenefit.coupon_3000.did_proportional.by_redemption.100.break_even_margin | pct0 }} under the proportional estimate. The 500 yen coupon needs {{ costbenefit.coupon_500.at_lower_edge.by_redemption.100.break_even_margin | pct0 }} to {{ costbenefit.coupon_500.at_lower_edge_diff.by_redemption.100.break_even_margin | pct0 }} at the lower edge and has no measurable effect at the upper edge. Whether a margin of that size, or a redemption rate low enough, applies is a question for the company's own figures; the table shows what has to be true.

